//
// AN OPERATOR ON A TUPLE OF THIS SOURCE'S RECORDS IS REFUSED (`@FR-Op-Def` crossed with
// `@FR-Op-Home`).  `(V, V)` is built from this source's `V`, but the tuple type itself is not
// declared here — it is built in — so `self: (V, V)` is not "of a user type" and the definition is
// refused where it is written, though nothing uses it.
//
// @falsified-at: none — a conformance guard; refused as the rule says, interpret and native.
// CHANNEL: the compile (the expected diagnostic).
// ARMED: no instrument.
// WITNESS: the diagnostic names the tuple type.
// HOLDS: `every-user-type-of-this-source-may-be-an-operators-self` beside it.
// @EXPECT_ERROR: `operator plus` defines `+` for a type of its own package, and `(V, V)` is not one

struct V { x: integer }

operator plus(self: (V, V), o: V) -> V { o }

fn main() {
  println("x");
}
